# ic.scup -- the Iced Coffee grammar.  THIS is your parser: each
# nonterminal of your grammar becomes a rule below, with an action
# block of Scala that builds your AST node from the production's
# symbols ($1, $2, ... over all symbols, $i.text/$i.line/$i.value on
# tokens).  Terminals in quotes come from ic.slex's %token table;
# bare names are token kinds or other rules.
#
#   rule ::= sym sym sym   { Node($1.text, $3, $1.line) }
#          | %empty        { ... }
#          ;
#
# Declare `%type rule: YourNodeType` as you design your AST: the
# action blocks are then statically checked against the grammar, with
# errors at the lines of this file.  The EBNF shorthands x?, x*, x+
# match the course handouts, and %list(x, sep) is a sep-separated
# list (x? produces an Option, the list forms produce List, and an
# un-blocked %empty produces None -- so such a rule's %type must be
# an Option).  A %left/%right block
# followed by `rule ::= %binary(operand) { ... }` builds a whole
# precedence ladder; %chain(base, suffixRule) runs a postfix loop
# with $$ bound to the expression built so far; %greedy before a rule
# resolves the dangling else.
#
# Three systematic changes from the "raw" grammar in the IC spec make
# it LL(1) (see the spec's LL(1) appendix, and HW 2):
#
#  - No left recursion.  Left-recursive rules such as
#      Expr ::= Expr '+' Expr
#    become a %binary precedence ladder or %chain postfix loops.
#
#  - Alternatives that share a prefix must be left-factored until one
#    token of lookahead decides.  The spec's appendix works two of
#    these for you (class members, `new`); the statement rule and
#    `primary` are yours -- when you get them wrong, the LL(1) check
#    rejects the grammar and names the conflicting productions.
#    (`sbt "runMain scup.check ic.scup"` checks the grammar alone.)
#
#  - The dangling else needs the one %greedy rule.
#
# TODO: replace the stub below with the full IC grammar, adding
# %type declarations as you design your AST nodes.

%start  program

# The stub grammar: a "program" is just the keyword `class`.
program ::= "class" ;
